<!-- Website teaching layer for android/os-internals/intent-resolution-and-manifest.md.
     The original chapter is rendered in full; everything here is added around it.
     Sources checked 2026-10-25: Intents and intent filters, <activity> android:exported, Android App Links
     verification, Package visibility, PendingIntent mutability guidance (developer.android.com). -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A customer taps <code>https://example.com/orders/43</code> in an email. Your verified App Link opens the order screen at once, with no chooser and no question. But order 43 belongs to someone else. The link reached exactly the right app, and the app still had to decide whether to show it.</p>

Every manifest entry is a promise about how other apps and the system may reach you. Every intent that arrives is input from outside. This chapter explains how Android matches an intent to a component and which manifest settings form your security boundary. The additions here separate what the system decides from what your code must still check.

<!-- @diagram b3a61092347dc92b -->
### How an implicit intent finds its target

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-flow"><div class="node"><strong>Caller app</strong><small><code>startActivity(intent)</code></small></div><div class="node"><strong>ActivityManagerService</strong><small>asks PackageManager to resolve</small></div><div class="node is-key"><strong>PackageManagerService</strong><small>matches action, data and category against filters</small></div><div class="node"><strong>ResolveInfo</strong><small>or chooser candidates</small></div><div class="node" data-state="done"><strong>Target app</strong><small>component launched via Binder</small></div></div><figcaption>Redrawn from the original sequence diagram. Matching happens in system services, outside both apps.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Manifest as Security Boundary :: visual -->
### Four gates, two owners

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-cols" style="--cols:2"><div class="col"><span class="col-title">The system decides</span><div class="node"><strong>1. Does a filter match?</strong><small>action, data and category against installed filters</small></div><div class="node"><strong>2. Which app gets the link?</strong><small>App Link verification ties a domain to your app</small></div></div><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Your code decides</span><div class="node is-key"><strong>3. Is it a known route?</strong><small>scheme, host, path and parameters parsed and validated</small></div><div class="node is-key"><strong>4. May this user see it?</strong><small>sign-in and server-side authorization</small></div></div></div><figcaption>Passing gates 1 and 2 means the intent reached you, not that it is safe or permitted. Gates 3 and 4 belong to the router.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Production Patterns :: animation -->
