<!-- @after Staff Engineer Thinking :: staff -->
Put the merged manifest in code review. A new exported component, intent filter, permission or `<queries>` entry changes your app's public surface, even when the Kotlin diff looks unrelated, because library manifests merge into yours.

<!-- @after Failure Modes :: check -->
### Check the boundary

<details><summary>Your App Link is verified. Does the router still need to validate the path and the user?</summary>

Yes. Verification decides which app opens the domain's links. It says nothing about whether this path is a known route or whether this user may see the content.

</details>

<details><summary>An internal debug activity has no intent filter. Should you still write <code>android:exported="false"</code>?</summary>

Without intent filters it is not exported by default, but writing it explicitly makes the intent visible in manifest review and protects you if someone adds a filter later.

</details>

<details><summary>A screen receives a full order with 500 line items as an extra. What fails, and what should you pass?</summary>

Extras cross Binder, so a large payload can fail with <code>TransactionTooLargeException</code>. Pass the order ID and let the destination load the data.

</details>

